**To find a lost or stolen Samsung Galaxy phone, open [samsungfind.samsung.com](https://samsungfind.samsung.com) in any browser and sign in with your Samsung account.** You'll see the phone on a map, and you can make it ring, lock it, track its location, extend its battery life or erase it. This works when the phone is switched on and has a connection. If it's switched off, Samsung shows only its last known location. Samsung Find tells you where the phone is, but never who has it.

## Samsung Find, SmartThings Find and Find My Mobile

Samsung uses three names, which is confusing:

- **Samsung Find** is the current app and website for finding your Galaxy devices.
- **SmartThings Find** is part of Samsung's SmartThings app. Samsung says it's a different app from Samsung Find, but you can use either one to locate your phone.
- **Find My Mobile** is a name you'll still see in the phone's own settings, under Lost device protection.

This guide uses Samsung Find.

## How to find your Samsung phone

You need another device with a browser: a computer, a tablet or someone else's phone.

1. Go to [samsungfind.samsung.com](https://samsungfind.samsung.com) and sign in with the Samsung account that's on your phone.
2. Your devices are listed on the left. Select the phone.
3. Its location appears on the map.

If you have another Galaxy phone or tablet, you can use the **Samsung Find app** on it instead. A family member can also find your phone in their Samsung Find app, but only if you shared its location with them beforehand.

**If the phone was stolen, don't go to the location yourself.** Samsung says the same: give the location to the police and let them recover it.

## What you can do from Samsung Find

- **Ring.** The phone plays a ringtone for one minute, even if it's set to mute or vibrate. Use this when it's somewhere nearby.
- **Lock.** You create a PIN and can add an emergency contact and a message for the lock screen, so a finder knows who to call. Samsung Find also has **Lost mode**, which Samsung says restricts Samsung Pay and Samsung Pass and prevents the phone from being turned off.
- **Track location.** You get an updated location every 15 minutes. Samsung suggests it for a phone left on a bus or train, or one that may have been stolen.
- **Extend battery life.** This turns on Maximum power saving mode remotely, so the phone stays on longer while you look for it.
- **Get notified when it's found.** If the phone can't be located right now, Samsung Find can notify you when it's detected again.
- **Erase data.** A remote factory reset. Samsung notes that an SD card in the phone is erased too, and that if a Google account was on the phone, Google device protection is activated, so the phone asks for that account after the reset. Treat this as the last step, when you're sure the phone isn't coming back.

Some of these, including Erase data, are only on the website and not in the app. If you can't erase the phone, Samsung recommends changing the passwords of the accounts that are signed in on it.

## Turn these on before you need them

Samsung Find only works if the phone was set up for it. Check this today:

**Go to:**
`Settings > Security and privacy > Lost device protection`

Turn on all three:

- **Allow this phone to be found.** Samsung says this is switched on by default when you add a Samsung account, but check.
- **Send last location.** The phone saves its last location to Samsung's server before it powers off.
- **Offline finding.** Other Galaxy devices nearby can detect your phone and report where it is, even when it has no mobile data or Wi-Fi.

Your Galaxy phone also has Google's Find Hub, which works separately from Samsung Find. Keep both on. See [how to find a stolen Android phone with Find Hub](/blog/find-stolen-android-phone).

## What Samsung Find can't do

- **Find a phone that's switched off.** Samsung says a powered-off phone shows only its last known location. Offline finding helps when the phone is on but not connected, not when it's off.
- **Work without a Samsung account.** If you never added one to the phone, there's nothing to sign in to.
- **Show you who has the phone.** You get a dot on a map. No photo, no name.
- **Tell you that someone is trying to get in.** There's no alert when a stranger enters wrong PINs on your lock screen.

A thief's first move is usually to switch the phone off or turn on airplane mode. See [how to stop power off and airplane mode from the lock screen](/blog/block-power-menu) to make that harder on a Galaxy.

## How to get a photo of the person who has your phone

A location on a map is useful. A photo of the person holding your phone, taken at that location, gives the police far more to go on.

That's what CrookCatcher adds. Someone who picks up a locked phone usually tries a PIN or two. Each time an unlock attempt fails, CrookCatcher takes a photo with the front camera, records the GPS location and an approximate address, and emails both to you. The email is in your inbox even if the phone is switched off a minute later. If the phone has no connection at that moment, the email goes out as soon as it reconnects.

